+++
title: Creativity
blurb: For writers, artists, and anyone rebuilding a creative practice.
hook: When did you last finish something you actually wanted to make?
hook2: Was it a lack of time, or a lack of permission?
+++

## The work nobody warned you about

Somewhere between deciding you wanted to make something and actually
sitting down to make it, a second job appeared — the job of getting
yourself to do the first job. Nobody warns you about this part. The
talent, the taste, the ideas: those were supposed to be the hard part.
Instead the hard part turns out to be the fifteen minutes before you
start, the eleven browser tabs that open themselves, the sudden, urgent
need to reorganize a closet that has been fine for six years.

This isn't a discipline problem, whatever it feels like at 11pm with an
empty page open. It's what happens when the part of you that makes things
runs into the part of you that's protecting you from being seen, judged,
or wrong in public — and creative work, almost uniquely among the things
adults do, insists on being seen, judged, and possibly wrong in public. A
tax return that's mediocre doesn't say anything about who you are. A
painting that's mediocre feels like it does.

## What actually gets in the way

Ask around and you'll hear the same handful of stories in different
costumes.

There's the **perfectionism story**: the work has to be good before
anyone sees it, so nobody sees it, so it's never tested against reality,
so it never gets the feedback that would make it better, so the bar for
"good enough to show" keeps rising in your head while the actual work
stays exactly where it was. Perfectionism doesn't protect the work. It
just protects you from finding out what the work actually is.

There's the **permission story**: somewhere along the way, being creative
got filed under "things I do if I have extra time and energy," behind the
job, the family, the errands, the reasonable adult obligations — and
there is never extra time and energy, so the creative thing waits for a
version of your life that isn't coming. The permission story is sneaky
because it looks like practicality. It rarely is. It's usually just fear
wearing a scheduling problem as a disguise.

There's the **comparison story**, which the internet has made considerably
worse: you make one thing, imperfectly, in the time you actually have, and
you compare it to everyone else's edited highlight reel, made by people
further along or better resourced or simply better at hiding the mess. You
lose that comparison every single time, because it isn't a fair fight, and
then you conclude something false about your own ability from a contest
that was rigged from the start.

And there's the **identity story**, the quietest and most stubborn one: if
you're not sure the work is any good, it's safer to not really try,
because a half-effort that fails doesn't tell you anything about your
actual ceiling, but a full effort that fails might. Staying slightly
disengaged from your own work is a way of never finding out something
you're afraid to know.

None of these are solved by more willpower. They're solved by someone
helping you see which story you're actually in, and then helping you do
something different about it.

## What coaching actually does

A creativity coach isn't a teacher. You don't come here to learn a craft
— that's an MFA, a workshop, a mentor who already does what you want to
do. You also don't come here for therapy, even though some of what
surfaces (fear, shame, old messages about who's "allowed" to be an
artist) sounds like therapy material. A coach doesn't process your past
with you. A coach works with where you are right now and helps you
decide, deliberately, what you do next — and then holds you to it.

Concretely, that tends to look like a few things:

**Naming the actual obstacle**, not the plausible-sounding one. "I don't
have time" is almost never the real obstacle. Neither is "I'm not
talented enough," even though it's the one that gets said out loud most
often. A good session gets underneath the cover story to whatever's
really running the show — and that alone changes what's worth working on.

**Building a practice that survives contact with a real week.** Most
creative advice is written for a version of your life that has more
margin in it than your life actually has. Coaching starts from your
actual week — the one with the job and the kid and the tired — and builds
something small enough to survive it. A practice you can sustain for ten
minutes a day beats an ambitious plan you abandon by Thursday, every
time, because sustained beats ambitious over any period longer than a
week.

**Making the work less precious, on purpose.** A lot of creative paralysis
comes from treating every attempt as if it's the only one you get.
Coaching often involves deliberately lowering the stakes of a given
session — a bad page, a bad sketch, a bad draft, on purpose, just to prove
the world doesn't end — because the fastest way out of perfectionism is
usually through a pile of things that weren't perfect.

**Accountability that isn't just a deadline.** Anyone can set you a
deadline. What's harder to get on your own is someone who notices the
pattern behind three missed check-ins in a row and asks about that,
instead of just moving the deadline again. That's the actual value of
another person in the room: not pressure, but attention.

**A second set of eyes on your own resistance.** You are, by definition,
the worst-positioned person to see your own blind spots about your own
work and your own avoidance of it. A coach who's watching your process
over time — not just judging the output — starts to see the shape of
your particular resistance: where it shows up, what it says, what tends
to move it. That pattern recognition is hard to build alone, because by
the time you notice you're avoiding something, you're usually already
three tabs deep into avoiding it.

## What this isn't for

It's worth being honest about the edges of this. If what you want is
craft instruction — how to actually structure a novel, how to use color
theory, how to get a better sound out of an instrument — that's a
teacher's job, and coaching here won't pretend otherwise. If what's
surfacing is closer to grief, trauma, or a mental health concern that
happens to be showing up in your relationship to creative work, that
deserves a therapist, and a good coach will say so rather than trying to
coach around it. And if you're already making the work you want to make,
at the pace you want to make it, at peace with how it's going — you
probably don't need this. This is for the gap between wanting to make
something and actually making it, not for people who've already closed
that gap on their own.

## What a session looks like

There's no fixed curriculum, which surprises some people at first. A
session might start with "I didn't do the thing I said I'd do" and spend
the whole hour on what actually happened in the fifteen minutes before
you didn't do it — because that fifteen minutes usually has more
information in it than a month of good intentions. Another session might
be almost entirely about a specific piece of work: what's not working,
what you're avoiding fixing, why. Another might not touch the work
directly at all, and instead deal with a story about identity or
permission that's been quietly running the show for years.

What stays consistent is the orientation: forward, not backward. Not "why
are you like this," but "given that this is where you are, what's the
next true thing to do about it." Coaching is less interested in your
creative history than in your creative next move.

## Who tends to come here

Writers with a manuscript that's been "almost done" for two years.
Painters and makers who used to have a practice and lost it somewhere in
the last decade of adult life. People who were creative as kids and were
told, gently or not, to be more practical, and are now wondering,
quietly, whether that advice was actually right for them. People who have
a successful creative practice by outside measures and still feel like
frauds every time they start something new. People who don't call
themselves artists at all, but keep returning to some form of making —
writing, music, cooking, building things with their hands — and want to
take it more seriously without knowing what "more seriously" would even
look like for them.

There's no entry requirement of talent, output, or credential. There is
one real requirement: an actual willingness to make something, even
imperfectly, in the time you have. Coaching can work with fear, with
doubt, with a decade of stalled starts. It has a much harder time with
someone who isn't willing to put anything on the page at all.

## Where this actually goes

Nobody comes out the other side of creativity coaching with a
fear-free relationship to making things — that isn't the goal, and anyone
promising it is selling something. What tends to actually change is
smaller and more durable than that: the gap between wanting to make
something and starting closes from weeks to hours. The bad first draft
stops being a referendum on your worth and starts being what a first
draft is supposed to be. The story you tell about why you haven't made
the thing gets a lot shorter, because most of the excuses turn out not to
survive being said out loud to someone who's actually listening.

If any of this sounds like your particular version of stuck, that's
exactly the kind of stuck this domain exists for.
